  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cloudy, rainy, and cool conditions continue\nthrough Wednesday, with a return to drier weather on Thursday.\n\nAs of 330 AM, current radar imagery shows shower activity\nexpanding in coverage across the forecast area. Most of this \nactivity is on the lighter side, but there are a few embedded \nheavier downpours. Shower activity will continue through the day\ntoday as large scale forcing for ascent continues to increase\ndownstream of an approaching shortwave disturbance/closed low\nover the Ohio Valley and within the right entrance region of a\ndeveloping jet streak to our north. An already well developed \nCAD wedge will continue to be reinforced by the rainfall and \nnortheasterly flow to the south of an unseasonably strong area \nof high pressure centered to our north across southern Quebec.\n\nCloudy skies, on and off showers, and well below normal\ntemperatures will continue through the day tomorrow. By the time\nall is said and done, most locations should receive around 1-2\ninches of rainfall, which should be beneficial in nature given\nthe ongoing drought conditions. Temperatures will run well below\nnormal, with highs both today and tomorrow in the upper 50s to\nmid 60s. Northeasterly winds gusting to around 15-30 mph will\nmake it feel even cooler.\n\nRain should come to an end first tomorrow morning across far \nnortheast Maryland, with the drier air making slow progress \ntoward the southwest tomorrow afternoon through tomorrow night.\nDrier conditions are expected for all on Thursday beneath mostly\ncloudy skies. Temperatures will remain well below normal, with\nhighs only in the 60s, and winds will stay gusty out of the\nnortheast as a coastal low starts to deepen offshore.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions and a gradual warming trend can be \nexpected through the weekend.\n\nA Rex block during the second half of the week transitions to \nmore of an Omega block over the weekend as a trough moving across \nthe Canadian Rockies results in ridge building across the Great \nLakes. At this time, it appears this subtle shift allows the closed \nupper low to drift a bit to the east, somewhere near or just off the \nNew England or Mid Atlantic coast. This likely keeps any coastal low \nthreat and rainfall threats off to the northeast from Friday through \nthe weekend. However, with inherently less predictability with \nblocky patterns, the end of the week still bears watching for any \nretrogression of the pattern. While some areas could see some sun \nThursday, greater chances for reduced cloud cover arrive areawide on \nFriday. Breezy winds will continue through Saturday, if not Sunday, \ndue to the pressure gradient between the coastal low near New \nEngland and surface high across the Great Lakes. The wind direction \nbetween northeast and northwest will help keep temperatures below \nnormal into the weekend, though gradually warming. Temperatures will \nbe closer to normal by Monday, as the low likely starts drifting \naway and the ridge builds overhead.",
